__d(function(e,t,i,r,o,a,n){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:12,chapter_title:"Electricity and Circuits",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"What is the minimum requirement for electric current to flow in a circuit?",options:["A) A switch","B) A closed (complete) path with a cell and a conductor","C) A bulb","D) A battery of three cells"],correct_answer:"B) A closed (complete) path with a cell and a conductor",explanation:"For current to flow, two things are essential: an energy source (cell/battery) to push the current, and a complete, unbroken conducting path (closed circuit) for the current to travel through. A switch and bulb are useful but not minimum requirements \u2014 current can flow through a simple wire connecting the cell's terminals (though this would cause a short circuit). The critical requirement is a CLOSED path \u2014 any break stops all current."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which terminal of a cell is the raised bump end?",options:["A) Negative terminal","B) Neutral terminal","C) Positive terminal","D) Ground terminal"],correct_answer:"C) Positive terminal",explanation:"The positive (+) terminal of a cell is the raised, pointed/bumped end. The negative (\u2212) terminal is the flat end. On a AA cell (pencil cell), you can identify the terminals by touch \u2014 one end is flat, the other has a small raised button. In a circuit, current (by convention) flows out of the positive terminal, through the external circuit, and into the negative terminal."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is a good conductor of electricity?",options:["A) Rubber","B) Glass","C) Dry wood","D) Copper"],correct_answer:"D) Copper",explanation:"Copper is an excellent conductor of electricity \u2014 it has free electrons that move easily when a voltage is applied. This is why copper is the most widely used metal for electrical wiring. Rubber, glass, and dry wood are all insulators \u2014 they have tightly bound electrons that do not allow current to pass through them. Copper's combination of high conductivity, flexibility, and relatively low cost makes it ideal for wires."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"When a switch in a circuit is open, what happens?",options:["A) Current flows at half speed","B) Current flows in reverse","C) No current flows; the circuit is incomplete","D) The cell discharges faster"],correct_answer:"C) No current flows; the circuit is incomplete",explanation:"An open switch creates a physical gap (break) in the conducting path. Since current cannot cross this gap (the gap is filled with air, which is an insulator), no current flows anywhere in the circuit \u2014 not just across the switch. The bulb goes off, the motor stops, all devices cease to work. Closing the switch restores the conducting path, allowing current to flow again."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"A student connects a bulb to a cell using wires. The bulb glows. She then touches the free probe ends of a tester circuit to a metal spoon. The bulb in the tester glows. What does this mean?",options:["A) The spoon is an insulator","B) The spoon is a conductor","C) The spoon is a semiconductor","D) The spoon is a source of electricity"],correct_answer:"B) The spoon is a conductor",explanation:"When the tester's bulb glows after touching the probes to the spoon, it means current is flowing through the spoon \u2014 completing the circuit. This proves the spoon is a conductor (it allows current to pass through it). If the spoon were an insulator, the circuit would remain open (the spoon would not let current pass) and the bulb would not glow."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is an insulator?",options:["A) Copper wire","B) Graphite (pencil lead)","C) Rubber eraser","D) Iron nail"],correct_answer:"C) Rubber eraser",explanation:"Rubber is an insulator \u2014 its tightly bound electrons do not allow current to flow. This is why electrical wires are coated in rubber or plastic (both insulators). Copper wire and iron nail are metals and excellent conductors. Graphite (pencil lead) is a non-metal but is also a conductor due to its unique electron structure \u2014 it is an exception to the rule that non-metals are insulators."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Electrical wires used in homes are covered with plastic or rubber because:",options:["A) Plastic and rubber are good conductors","B) Plastic and rubber are insulators that prevent electric shock and short circuits","C) Plastic is cheaper than copper","D) Rubber makes the wire flexible"],correct_answer:"B) Plastic and rubber are insulators that prevent electric shock and short circuits",explanation:"The primary reason for coating wires with plastic/rubber is safety. Both materials are insulators \u2014 they prevent current from flowing through them. The insulating coat: (1) prevents accidental electric shock if someone touches the wire; (2) prevents short circuits when two wires touch each other. While rubber also adds flexibility and plastic reduces cost, the fundamental reason for the coating is electrical insulation and safety."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Karnataka's electricity generation includes power from the Sharavathi river. What type of power plant is this?",options:["A) Thermal power plant","B) Solar power plant","C) Hydroelectric power plant","D) Nuclear power plant"],correct_answer:"C) Hydroelectric power plant",explanation:"The Sharavathi Hydroelectric Project in Shivamogga district, Karnataka, uses the flow of the Sharavathi river (Jog Falls) to generate electricity. Water from the reservoir turns turbines which generate electricity \u2014 this is hydroelectric (hydro = water) power. Karnataka also has the Raichur Thermal Power Station (coal-based), Pavagada Solar Park (solar), and Kaiga Nuclear Power Plant. The Sharavathi project has an installed capacity of about 1,035 MW."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A torch has two cells. If one cell is reversed (negative terminal facing the wrong direction), what will happen?",options:["A) The torch will glow twice as bright","B) The torch will not glow or will glow very dimly","C) The torch will glow normally","D) The reversed cell will protect the other cell"],correct_answer:"B) The torch will not glow or will glow very dimly",explanation:"In a series circuit (which is how torch cells are connected), the cells must be aligned with positive terminal facing the negative terminal of the next cell (+ to \u2212). If one cell is reversed (+ facing +, or \u2212 facing \u2212), the two cells oppose each other \u2014 the reversed cell's voltage works against the other. In a 2-cell torch, both cells cancel out (net voltage = 0) and the torch does not glow. Correct alignment is crucial for current to flow in the right direction and sum up properly."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following correctly describes a short circuit?",options:["A) A circuit with a very long wire","B) A direct connection between + and \u2212 terminals without any load, causing excessive current","C) A circuit with too many bulbs","D) A circuit where the switch is always open"],correct_answer:"B) A direct connection between + and \u2212 terminals without any load, causing excessive current",explanation:"A short circuit occurs when a low-resistance path directly connects the positive and negative terminals of a power source without any load (bulb, resistor, motor) in between. This causes an extremely large current to flow, generating intense heat in the wire and cell. Short circuits can cause fire, explosion of the cell, and damage to appliances. Fuses and circuit breakers are designed to break the circuit before a short circuit causes serious damage."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"In which type of circuit do all components stop working if one bulb fuses?",options:["A) Parallel circuit","B) Series circuit","C) Both series and parallel","D) Neither"],correct_answer:"B) Series circuit",explanation:"In a series circuit, all components share a single conducting path. If one bulb's filament breaks (fuses), the path is broken and no current flows anywhere \u2014 all bulbs go off. This is why old Christmas light strings (wired in series) went completely dark if one bulb failed. In a parallel circuit, each bulb has its own independent path \u2014 one fusing does not affect the others. This is why homes use parallel wiring: one circuit fault does not affect other circuits."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A student tests various materials with a circuit tester (cell + bulb + probes). She finds the bulb glows when she touches the probes to a pencil's graphite tip. What conclusion can she draw?",options:["A) Carbon/graphite is an insulator like other non-metals","B) Carbon/graphite is a conductor even though it is a non-metal","C) The pencil contains metal inside","D) The cell is producing heat, not electricity"],correct_answer:"B) Carbon/graphite is a conductor even though it is a non-metal",explanation:"Graphite (the material in pencil 'lead') is a form of carbon \u2014 a non-metal. However, unlike most non-metals, graphite has a layered structure where one electron per carbon atom is free to move, making it an electrical conductor. The bulb glowing proves current flows through the graphite. This is an important exception: not all non-metals are insulators. Graphite is used as electrodes in batteries and electrolysis because of this conductivity."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Why should you NEVER conduct electricity experiments with household AC current (wall sockets)?",options:["A) AC current is too weak to power a bulb","B) Wall sockets use 230V AC, which is powerful enough to cause fatal electric shock","C) AC current does not flow through bulbs","D) AC current only works in factories, not homes"],correct_answer:"B) Wall sockets use 230V AC, which is powerful enough to cause fatal electric shock",explanation:"India's household electricity is 230V AC (alternating current at 50Hz). At this voltage, if current passes through the human body (which is a conductor), it can cause severe burns, muscle paralysis, cardiac arrest, and death. School experiments must use cells and batteries (1.5V to 9V DC) \u2014 at these low voltages, the human body's electrical resistance is high enough to limit current to a safe level. Never experiment with, repair, or touch household wiring \u2014 always use a licensed electrician."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"What is the function of a fuse in an electric circuit?",options:["A) To increase the current in the circuit","B) To store electrical energy","C) To protect the circuit by melting when excess current flows","D) To convert AC to DC"],correct_answer:"C) To protect the circuit by melting when excess current flows",explanation:"A fuse is a safety device made of a thin wire with a low melting point. When current exceeds a safe limit (due to short circuit or overload), the fuse wire heats up rapidly and melts \u2014 breaking the circuit and stopping the current before it can damage appliances or cause fire. The fuse sacrifices itself to protect the rest of the circuit. Modern homes use MCBs (Miniature Circuit Breakers) which trip and can be reset, replacing older replaceable fuses."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"In Karnataka's power distribution system, the voltage of electricity is reduced (stepped down) before reaching homes. Why?",options:["A) High voltage uses more electricity and costs more","B) Appliances are designed for 230V; high transmission voltage (220,000V) would destroy them","C) Low voltage travels farther than high voltage","D) Stepping down creates more electricity"],correct_answer:"B) Appliances are designed for 230V; high transmission voltage (220,000V) would destroy them",explanation:"Electricity is transmitted at very high voltages (220 kV = 220,000V) over long distances because high voltage reduces energy loss in transmission wires. However, home appliances operate at 230V \u2014 connecting them to 220,000V would destroy them instantly and is fatally dangerous. Transformers in substations step down the voltage progressively: 220,000V \u2192 11,000V \u2192 415V \u2192 230V (for homes). KPTCL (Karnataka Power Transmission Corporation) manages this step-down network."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A circuit has a cell, a bulb, a switch, and connecting wires. The switch is closed but the bulb does not glow. Which of the following is NOT a possible reason?",options:["A) The bulb's filament is broken","B) The cell is discharged","C) One wire connection is loose","D) The switch is made of copper"],correct_answer:"D) The switch is made of copper",explanation:"Copper is a conductor \u2014 a switch made of copper would conduct electricity fine when closed. This would not prevent the bulb from glowing. The actual possible reasons for the bulb not glowing are: broken filament (opens the circuit within the bulb), discharged cell (no energy source), or loose connection (creates a break in the circuit path). The switch material (if conducting) does not prevent current flow when the switch is closed."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Salt water conducts electricity, but pure (distilled) water does not. Why?",options:["A) Salt makes water heavier, so it conducts better","B) Dissolved salt breaks into ions (charged particles) in water, which carry electric charge","C) Pure water is transparent and transparent materials do not conduct","D) Salt contains metal, which conducts electricity"],correct_answer:"B) Dissolved salt breaks into ions (charged particles) in water, which carry electric charge",explanation:"Electric current is the flow of charged particles. Pure water has no free charges \u2014 its molecules are neutral. When salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) dissolves in water, it separates into positive sodium ions (Na+) and negative chloride ions (Cl\u2212). These ions are free to move and carry electric charge \u2014 making salt water a conductor. Pure water with no dissolved ions has no free charge carriers and does not conduct. This is why drinking water (which has dissolved minerals) conducts, but chemically pure water does not."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Two identical bulbs are connected \u2014 first in series with one cell, then in parallel with the same cell. In which case will each bulb glow brighter?",options:["A) Series \u2014 because both bulbs share the current","B) Parallel \u2014 because each bulb receives the full cell voltage independently","C) Both cases are identical in brightness","D) Series \u2014 because current is doubled in series"],correct_answer:"B) Parallel \u2014 because each bulb receives the full cell voltage independently",explanation:"In a parallel circuit, each bulb is connected directly across the cell \u2014 so each bulb receives the full 1.5V from the cell. In a series circuit, the cell's 1.5V is shared between the two bulbs \u2014 each receives about 0.75V. Lower voltage means less current through each bulb, meaning less brightness. Therefore, each bulb in parallel glows at full brightness, while each bulb in series glows more dimly. This is why home appliances are wired in parallel \u2014 each gets full supply voltage."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A student connects a circuit with a cell (1.5V), a wire, and no bulb or load. The wire gets very hot quickly. What has happened and why?",options:["A) The cell is malfunctioning and heating the wire","B) A short circuit \u2014 direct connection allows very large current, generating heat","C) The wire is too long and current slows down, generating heat","D) The cell voltage is too low for the wire"],correct_answer:"B) A short circuit \u2014 direct connection allows very large current, generating heat",explanation:"Connecting a wire directly from + to \u2212 of a cell with no load creates a short circuit. With no resistance (like a bulb) in the path, the circuit's resistance is very low \u2014 so by Ohm's Law (V = IR), a very large current flows. This large current generates significant heat in the wire (Q = I\xb2Rt). In more extreme cases (higher voltage), the wire melts, the cell overheats and may leak or explode, and fire can result. Always include a load (bulb, resistor) in a circuit to limit current."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Karnataka's Pavagada Solar Park converts sunlight into electricity. The electricity produced is DC (direct current). But homes use AC (alternating current). What device converts DC to AC for home use?",options:["A) A transformer","B) A fuse","C) An inverter","D) A capacitor"],correct_answer:"C) An inverter",explanation:"Solar panels produce DC (direct current) \u2014 current flows in one direction. Homes and the grid use AC (alternating current) \u2014 current reverses direction 50 times per second (50Hz in India). An inverter is the device that converts DC to AC. At Pavagada Solar Park (Tumakuru, Karnataka), large inverters convert the panels' DC output to 415V AC for grid injection. Transformers then step up the voltage for long-distance transmission. At home, a UPS/inverter connected to a battery does the same \u2014 converts DC from the battery to AC for household appliances during a power cut."}]}},2722,[]);